Title: The Harbor Light
Author: Prototype sample

FADE IN:

EXT. FISHING HARBOR - DAWN

Fog sits low on the water. A line of wooden boats knocks gently against the pier, ropes creaking, gulls circling a crate of yesterday's catch that nobody bothered to move. Somewhere behind the fish market a radio plays a song nobody has requested in thirty years, and the tide pulls at the pilings with the patience of something that has all the time in the world. [[Is this too long for an opening image? Try cutting the radio.]]

SUPER: SAINT-MARC, HAITI. 2019.

NADIA (30s), oilskin jacket, hair tied back with a shoelace, walks the length of the pier with a thermos in one hand and a ledger tucked under her arm. She counts the boats under her breath.

She stops at the last mooring. It's empty.

NADIA
Seven. There should be eight.

TOMAS (60s), the harbormaster, leans out of a weather-beaten hut at the end of the pier.

TOMAS
(not looking up)
The Esperanza went out at four. Your brother took her.

NADIA
In this? You can't see the end of your own arm out there.

TOMAS
I told him that. He said the fish don't care about the fog, and neither does the bank. [[Tomas should sound tired here, not cruel.]]

Nadia sets the thermos down on a bollard. She opens the ledger, runs her finger down a column of figures, and closes it again, harder than she needs to.

NADIA
(quietly)
How much does he owe them?

TOMAS
More than the boat is worth. Less than the boat is worth to him.

NADIA
That isn't an answer.

TOMAS
It's the only one I have that won't make you cry on my pier.

A long, low horn sounds somewhere out in the fog. Both of them turn toward it.

NADIA
Is that him?

TOMAS
It's the lighthouse.

NADIA ^
It's been broken since Tuesday!

TOMAS
Then it isn't the lighthouse.

Tomas doesn't move for a long moment. He looks past her, out at the place where the breakwater should be, where on a clear morning you can see the whole bay laid out like a map, the reefs and the channel markers and the lighthouse on its little island of black rock. This morning there is only gray, and the gray is moving. A gull lands on the roof of the hut, considers the two of them, and leaves again without comment, as if it has seen this conversation before and knows how it ends.

He steps out of the hut, pulls on a cap, and starts untying the small skiff tied beneath the pier, his fingers working the wet knots from memory, never once looking down at them.

TOMAS
When your father was alive, he used to say the sea keeps a ledger too, and it never forgets a debt. I thought he was being dramatic. He was always being dramatic. But I have watched forty years of men go out past that breakwater thinking they were the exception, thinking the fog would lift for them because they were owed something, and I have watched the sea collect from every single one of them. Your brother is not the exception. Nobody is the exception. So you can stand here and count boats, or you can get in, and we can go and find out what the sea thinks he owes. Those are the choices. I'm not going to make them prettier for you.

Nadia looks back at the town, the lights coming on one by one in the windows up the hill, then down at the skiff rocking in the black water.

She climbs in.

CUT TO:

EXT. OPEN WATER - CONTINUOUS

The skiff cuts through the fog. Tomas steers by feel, one hand on the tiller, eyes half closed, listening. Nadia sits in the bow with a flashlight that lights up nothing but more fog, the beam breaking apart a few feet from the boat like it's afraid to go any further.

NADIA
How do you know where you're going?

TOMAS
I don't. I know where the rocks are. Everything else is a guess.

Somewhere ahead, faint, the sound of an engine coughing and dying, coughing and dying.

NADIA
(standing)
Mateo! MATEO!

MATEO (O.S.)
Nadia?

NADIA
Stay where you are! Keep talking!

MATEO (O.S.)
I've been talking for an hour! There's nobody to talk to out here but the fish, and they've heard all my stories.

TOMAS
(under his breath)
He's fine. Only the living complain that much.

The engine sound stops. The fog thins for a moment and there she is: the ESPERANZA, listing to one side, her nets tangled in the propeller, MATEO (20s) waving from the stern with both arms.

SMASH CUT TO:

INT. HARBORMASTER'S HUT - LATER

A radio crackles on the shelf. Rain streaks the single window. Nadia sits wrapped in a blanket, hands around a mug she isn't drinking from. Mateo stands by the stove, dripping, trying to look like a man who meant to do all of it.

MATEO
I was going to pay them back.

NADIA
With what? The fish you didn't catch?

MATEO
With the fish I would have caught if the engine hadn't --

NADIA
Stop. Just stop. I don't want the version where you were almost right.

FADE OUT.
